CHECK cannot express a cross-row requirement that every team retains an admin; a BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE trigger enforces the invariant for single-session writes; a materialized view audits gaps; SERIALIZABLE or SELECT FOR UPDATE is required against concurrent write skew.

Complete runnable script plus demos. Save it back over the `setup.sql` that `init` copied and apply it to the running lab with `docker exec -i lab-ch8-p1 psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -U postgres -d ch8_txns < setup.sql`; the `ch8_txns` database and its seed rows come from the image.

```sql
-- ch8_team_admins.sql
DROP MATERIALIZED VIEW IF EXISTS teams_without_admins;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS team_members CASCADE;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS teams CASCADE;
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS check_team_admin_coverage();

CREATE TABLE teams (
  id   SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  name TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE team_members (
  user_id INT  NOT NULL,
  team_id INT  REFERENCES teams(id),
  role    TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (role IN ('admin', 'member')),
  PRIMARY KEY (user_id, team_id)
);

INSERT INTO teams VALUES (1, 'Engineering');
INSERT INTO team_members VALUES
  (10, 1, 'admin'),
  (11, 1, 'admin'),
  (12, 1, 'member');

-- Task 1: CHECK cannot reference other rows / use subqueries in a reliable way.
-- Running the following fails, e.g.:
--   ERROR:  cannot use subquery in check constraint
-- (PostgreSQL CHECK expressions must be immutable row-local predicates.)
-- ALTER TABLE team_members ADD CONSTRAINT at_least_one_admin CHECK (
--   (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM team_members tm
--    WHERE tm.team_id = team_id AND tm.role = 'admin') >= 1
-- );

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION check_team_admin_coverage()
RETURNS TRIGGER LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  remaining_admins INT;
  chk_team_id INT;
  chk_user_id INT;
BEGIN
  IF TG_OP = 'DELETE' THEN
    chk_team_id := OLD.team_id;
    chk_user_id := OLD.user_id;
    IF OLD.role <> 'admin' THEN RETURN OLD; END IF;
  ELSE
    chk_team_id := NEW.team_id;
    chk_user_id := NEW.user_id;
    IF NOT (NEW.role <> 'admin' AND OLD.role = 'admin') THEN RETURN NEW; END IF;
  END IF;

  SELECT COUNT(*) INTO remaining_admins
  FROM team_members
  WHERE team_id = chk_team_id
    AND role = 'admin'
    AND user_id <> chk_user_id;

  IF remaining_admins = 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION
      'Cannot remove last admin from team %', chk_team_id;
  END IF;

  IF TG_OP = 'DELETE' THEN RETURN OLD; ELSE RETURN NEW; END IF;
END;
$$;

CREATE TRIGGER team_admin_coverage_check
BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE ON team_members
FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION check_team_admin_coverage();

CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW teams_without_admins AS
SELECT t.id AS team_id, t.name
FROM teams t
LEFT JOIN team_members m ON m.team_id = t.id AND m.role = 'admin'
GROUP BY t.id, t.name
HAVING COUNT(m.user_id) = 0;
```

Verify the trigger (expected signals: exception when removing the last admin; success when another admin remains):

```sql
-- Succeeds: another admin (11) remains
UPDATE team_members SET role = 'member' WHERE user_id = 10 AND team_id = 1;

-- Restore for the next check
UPDATE team_members SET role = 'admin' WHERE user_id = 10 AND team_id = 1;

-- Fails once only one admin is left:
UPDATE team_members SET role = 'member' WHERE user_id = 11 AND team_id = 1;
UPDATE team_members SET role = 'member' WHERE user_id = 10 AND team_id = 1;
-- ERROR: Cannot remove last admin from team 1

-- Restore both admins
UPDATE team_members SET role = 'admin' WHERE user_id IN (10, 11) AND team_id = 1;

-- DELETE: non-last admin ok; last admin blocked
DELETE FROM team_members WHERE user_id = 10 AND team_id = 1;
-- Restore
INSERT INTO team_members VALUES (10, 1, 'admin');
DELETE FROM team_members WHERE user_id = 11 AND team_id = 1;
DELETE FROM team_members WHERE user_id = 10 AND team_id = 1;
-- ERROR: Cannot remove last admin from team 1
```

For concurrent write skew — two admins both observe at least two admins and downgrade simultaneously — the trigger alone is insufficient under snapshot isolation. Both sessions can pass the count check before either commits:

```sql
-- Option 1: SERIALIZABLE isolation (SSI detects the read-write conflict)
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM team_members
WHERE team_id = 1 AND role = 'admin';
-- If count >= 2, proceed:
UPDATE team_members SET role = 'member'
WHERE user_id = 10 AND team_id = 1;
COMMIT;
-- PostgreSQL SSI aborts the second concurrent transaction (SQLSTATE 40001).
```

```sql
-- Option 2: SELECT FOR UPDATE (locks existing admin rows)
BEGIN;
SELECT * FROM team_members
WHERE team_id = 1 AND role = 'admin'
FOR UPDATE;
-- Count the locked rows; if > 1, proceed.
UPDATE team_members SET role = 'member'
WHERE user_id = 10 AND team_id = 1;
COMMIT;
```

Key insight: the trigger enforces the invariant for single-session sequential writes. For concurrent sessions under snapshot isolation, both transactions can pass the count check before either commits — the anomaly occurs because both concurrent transactions validate against overlapping snapshots. SERIALIZABLE isolation or SELECT FOR UPDATE is required to prevent it. The materialized view is a read-only audit snapshot; refresh it explicitly (`REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW teams_without_admins`) and do not rely on it as a transactional guard inside the modifying transaction.

UNIQUE constraint for the uniqueness invariant + SERIALIZABLE for more complex conditions + comparison.

Complete runnable `setup.sql` with the UNIQUE constraint already applied. Save it back over the file `init` copied and apply it to the running lab with `docker exec -i lab-ch8-p3 psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -U postgres -d ch8_seats < setup.sql`; the `ch8_seats` database comes from the image.

```sql
-- setup.sql — flight seat booking (finished)
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS seat_reservations;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS flights;

CREATE TABLE flights (
  id    SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  route TEXT   NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE seat_reservations (
  id        SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  flight_id INT  NOT NULL REFERENCES flights(id),
  seat_no   TEXT NOT NULL,
  user_id   INT  NOT NULL,
  CONSTRAINT unique_seat_per_flight UNIQUE (flight_id, seat_no)
);

INSERT INTO flights VALUES (1, 'NYC-LON');
```

Task 1 — reproduce double-booking under REPEATABLE READ (both inserts succeed; two rows for seat 14A). For this demo, drop and recreate without the UNIQUE constraint (or comment it out), then:

```sql
-- Reset between attempts if needed:
-- DELETE FROM seat_reservations WHERE flight_id = 1 AND seat_no = '14A';

-- Session A
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
SELECT * FROM seat_reservations
WHERE flight_id = 1 AND seat_no = '14A';
-- empty (0 rows) — pause; switch to Session B

-- Session B
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
SELECT * FROM seat_reservations
WHERE flight_id = 1 AND seat_no = '14A';
-- empty (0 rows) — pause; switch to Session A

-- Session A
INSERT INTO seat_reservations (flight_id, seat_no, user_id)
VALUES (1, '14A', 101);
COMMIT;
-- succeeds — pause; switch to Session B

-- Session B
INSERT INTO seat_reservations (flight_id, seat_no, user_id)
VALUES (1, '14A', 102);
COMMIT;
-- also succeeds: phantom write skew / double-booking

SELECT * FROM seat_reservations WHERE flight_id = 1 AND seat_no = '14A';
-- two rows: user_id 101 and 102 for the same seat
```

Task 2 — why SELECT FOR UPDATE does not help:

SELECT ... FOR UPDATE locks rows that already exist and match the query. When the seat is free, the check returns zero rows, so there is nothing to lock. Session B's concurrent INSERT of a new matching row is a phantom — it changes the result set of A's earlier SELECT without touching any row A locked. FOR UPDATE therefore cannot block the conflicting INSERT; predicate/range locks or SERIALIZABLE (SSI), or a UNIQUE constraint that materializes the conflict, are required instead.

Task 3 — UNIQUE constraint (preferred here) vs SERIALIZABLE. With the finished setup above already containing `CONSTRAINT unique_seat_per_flight UNIQUE (flight_id, seat_no)`:

```sql
-- UNIQUE constraint approach (preferred for this scenario):
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO seat_reservations (flight_id, seat_no, user_id)
VALUES (1, '14A', 101);
-- No SELECT needed — let the constraint be the guard.
-- One session succeeds; the other gets a unique violation.
COMMIT;

-- SERIALIZABLE approach (preferred when invariant is more complex
-- and cannot be expressed as uniqueness):
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM seat_reservations
WHERE flight_id = 1 AND seat_no = '14A';
-- 0: proceed
INSERT INTO seat_reservations (flight_id, seat_no, user_id)
VALUES (1, '14A', 101);
COMMIT;
-- PostgreSQL SSI detects that B read data that A's insert changed
-- and aborts B with SQLSTATE 40001
```

Comparison:
- UNIQUE constraint: zero runtime overhead, enforced at storage level, catches the collision without any application-level check. It works only for invariants that prohibit duplicate values across a chosen set of columns. It cannot model aggregate limits such as three reservations per user per flight — for those, SERIALIZABLE is needed.
- SERIALIZABLE (SSI): handles any invariant, including multi-row aggregates. Requires application-level retry on SQLSTATE 40001. Has bookkeeping overhead proportional to transaction volume.

General rule: prefer constraints (UNIQUE, CHECK, FK) as the first line of defense — they are declarative, always enforced, and have no retry logic. Use SERIALIZABLE when the invariant cannot be expressed as a constraint.

For the booking scenario, the cleanest production pattern skips the pre-check entirely:

```sql
-- Optimistic: just try to insert, catch unique violation
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO seat_reservations (flight_id, seat_no, user_id)
VALUES (1, '14A', 101)
ON CONFLICT ON CONSTRAINT unique_seat_per_flight DO NOTHING;
-- Check rows affected: 0 = seat taken, 1 = booked
COMMIT;
```

Comment: 'Materializing conflicts' is the general name for this pattern — introducing a concrete object (here, the unique row) that can be locked or constrained to prevent phantom write skew. It is called a last resort when no other approach fits, but when a UNIQUE constraint naturally models the invariant, it is in fact the cleanest solution.

Unsafe split writes leave a deducted balance with no redemption row; the same writes inside one transaction roll back together; `redeem_points` locks, validates, then writes atomically. Serial-execution engines need the whole logic in one round trip so the single thread is not idle on the network.

Task 1 — unsafe non-transactional path (run statements one after another, no BEGIN):

```sql
-- Assume seed: user 1 has 500 points, no redemptions (psql autocommit on)
UPDATE loyalty_accounts
SET points = points - 100
WHERE user_id = 1;
-- points now 400 and already committed

DO $$ BEGIN RAISE EXCEPTION 'simulated crash after deduct'; END $$;
-- ERROR: simulated crash after deduct

INSERT INTO redemptions (user_id, points_used)
VALUES (1, 100);
-- never reached after the ERROR (skip or leave unrun)

SELECT points FROM loyalty_accounts WHERE user_id = 1;  -- 400
SELECT * FROM redemptions WHERE user_id = 1;             -- 0 rows
```

Task 2 — same writes wrapped in one transaction:

```sql
-- Reset first
UPDATE loyalty_accounts SET points = 500 WHERE user_id = 1;
DELETE FROM redemptions WHERE user_id = 1;

BEGIN;
UPDATE loyalty_accounts
SET points = points - 100
WHERE user_id = 1;

DO $$ BEGIN RAISE EXCEPTION 'simulated crash after deduct'; END $$;
-- ERROR aborts the transaction; both writes are undone

INSERT INTO redemptions (user_id, points_used)
VALUES (1, 100);
COMMIT;

SELECT points FROM loyalty_accounts WHERE user_id = 1;  -- 500
SELECT * FROM redemptions WHERE user_id = 1;             -- 0 rows
```

Task 3 — stored procedure with explicit locking, validation, and error handling:

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION redeem_points(
  p_user_id INT,
  p_points  INT
) RETURNS VOID LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  current_points INT;
BEGIN
  IF p_points <= 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Redemption amount must be positive, got %', p_points;
  END IF;

  -- Lock the account row to prevent concurrent redemptions from
  -- both reading the same balance and both passing the check
  SELECT points INTO current_points
  FROM loyalty_accounts
  WHERE user_id = p_user_id
  FOR UPDATE;

  IF current_points IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Loyalty account % not found', p_user_id;
  END IF;

  IF current_points < p_points THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION
      'Insufficient points for user %: balance % < requested %',
      p_user_id, current_points, p_points;
  END IF;

  UPDATE loyalty_accounts
  SET points = points - p_points
  WHERE user_id = p_user_id;

  INSERT INTO redemptions (user_id, points_used)
  VALUES (p_user_id, p_points);
END;
$$;

-- Usage
SELECT redeem_points(1, 100);
SELECT * FROM loyalty_accounts WHERE user_id = 1;  -- 400
SELECT * FROM redemptions WHERE user_id = 1;        -- 1 row

-- Test insufficient balance
SELECT redeem_points(2, 500);
-- ERROR: Insufficient points for user 2: balance 80 < requested 500
```

Task 4 — why VoltDB and Redis require stored procedures / scripts:

In interactive client/server style, a lot of time is spent in network communication between the application and the database. If you disallow concurrency and process only one transaction at a time, throughput is dreadful because the database spends most of its time waiting for the application to issue the next query.

A redemption takes three network round trips in interactive mode:
1. SELECT for balance check
2. UPDATE to deduct
3. INSERT for redemption record

With serial execution, each round trip is idle time — the single transaction thread sits blocked on network I/O. Systems with single-threaded serial transaction processing don't allow interactive multistatement transactions. Instead, the application must either limit itself to transactions containing a single statement or submit the entire transaction code to the database ahead of time, as a stored procedure.

By bundling all three steps into one stored procedure, the serial executor runs the entire redemption without ever waiting for a network response. VoltDB uses Java/Groovy; Redis uses Lua scripts (EVAL). PostgreSQL's stored procedures can serve the same role when designing for high-throughput serialized workloads.

Comment: The FOR UPDATE in the procedure prevents the lost update race: two concurrent redemption requests both reading the same balance, both passing the check, and together over-redeeming. Without it, a user with 100 points could trigger two 80-point redemptions simultaneously — both see 100, both check 100 >= 80, both proceed, and the CHECK constraint fires only after the balance hits -60.

SERIALIZABLE with a manual `psql` retry after SQLSTATE 40001, plus SELECT FOR UPDATE on the budget row as an alternative that serializes approvals without SSI aborts.

Reset before each demo:

```sql
UPDATE expense_approvals SET approved = false;
```

Task 1 — full two-session REPEATABLE READ write skew (final approved sum = 11000 > 10000):

Session 1:
```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
SELECT COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0) AS approved_total
FROM expense_approvals
WHERE project_id = 1 AND approved = true;
-- returns 0
SELECT amount FROM expense_approvals WHERE id = 1;
-- 6000
SELECT total_budget FROM project_budgets WHERE project_id = 1;
-- 10000; 0 + 6000 <= 10000 — pause here until Session 2 has also read the sum
UPDATE expense_approvals SET approved = true WHERE id = 1;
COMMIT;
```

Session 2 (interleaved — both SUM reads happen before either UPDATE):
```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
SELECT COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0) AS approved_total
FROM expense_approvals
WHERE project_id = 1 AND approved = true;
-- also returns 0 (snapshot from transaction start)
SELECT amount FROM expense_approvals WHERE id = 2;
-- 5000; 0 + 5000 <= 10000
UPDATE expense_approvals SET approved = true WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;
```

Verify the anomaly:
```sql
SELECT COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0) AS approved_total
FROM expense_approvals
WHERE project_id = 1 AND approved = true;
-- 11000 > 10000 budget — write skew
```

Task 2 — full SERIALIZABLE session SQL (Session 1 wins; Session 2 hits SSI):

Session 1:
```sql
UPDATE expense_approvals SET approved = false;

BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0) AS approved_total
FROM expense_approvals
WHERE project_id = 1 AND approved = true;
-- 0
SELECT amount FROM expense_approvals WHERE id = 1;
SELECT total_budget FROM project_budgets WHERE project_id = 1;
-- 0 + 6000 <= 10000
UPDATE expense_approvals SET approved = true WHERE id = 1;
COMMIT;
```

Session 2 (started before Session 1 commits, same check for id=2):
```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0) AS approved_total
FROM expense_approvals
WHERE project_id = 1 AND approved = true;
-- 0 on its snapshot
SELECT amount FROM expense_approvals WHERE id = 2;
SELECT total_budget FROM project_budgets WHERE project_id = 1;
UPDATE expense_approvals SET approved = true WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;
-- ERROR: could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions
-- SQLSTATE 40001 — retry the whole transaction
```

After Session 1 committed and Session 2 aborted, manually retry Session 2 in `psql` (required): start a fresh SERIALIZABLE transaction, re-check the sum, and refuse (`6000 + 5000 > 10000`):

```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0) AS approved_total
FROM expense_approvals
WHERE project_id = 1 AND approved = true;
-- 6000
SELECT amount FROM expense_approvals WHERE id = 2;
-- 5000; 6000 + 5000 > 10000 — do not UPDATE; ROLLBACK
ROLLBACK;
```

Optional: application retry loop around the SERIALIZABLE check-then-approve path (not required for the task):

```python
import time
import psycopg2
from psycopg2.errors import SerializationFailure

# assumes conn is an open psycopg2 connection; project_id / expense_id are ints
# autocommit=True so BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE actually starts
# the SSI transaction (with autocommit=False, the first execute already began a
# READ COMMITTED tx and that BEGIN would only warn).
conn.autocommit = True
MAX_RETRIES = 5
for attempt in range(MAX_RETRIES):
    try:
        with conn.cursor() as cur:
            cur.execute("BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE")
            cur.execute(
                """
                SELECT COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0)
                FROM expense_approvals
                WHERE project_id = %s AND approved = true
                """,
                [project_id],
            )
            already_approved = cur.fetchone()[0]
            cur.execute(
                "SELECT total_budget FROM project_budgets WHERE project_id = %s",
                [project_id],
            )
            budget = cur.fetchone()[0]
            cur.execute(
                "SELECT amount FROM expense_approvals WHERE id = %s",
                [expense_id],
            )
            this_amount = cur.fetchone()[0]
            if already_approved + this_amount > budget:
                cur.execute("ROLLBACK")
                raise ValueError("Approval would exceed project budget")
            cur.execute(
                "UPDATE expense_approvals SET approved = true WHERE id = %s",
                [expense_id],
            )
            cur.execute("COMMIT")
        break
    except SerializationFailure:
        with conn.cursor() as cur:
            cur.execute("ROLLBACK")
        if attempt == MAX_RETRIES - 1:
            raise
        time.sleep(2 ** attempt * 0.05)
```

Task 3 — full SELECT FOR UPDATE on `project_budgets` (second session waits, then refuses):

Session 1:
```sql
UPDATE expense_approvals SET approved = false;

BEGIN;
SELECT total_budget FROM project_budgets
WHERE project_id = 1
FOR UPDATE;
SELECT COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0) AS approved_total
FROM expense_approvals
WHERE project_id = 1 AND approved = true;
-- 0
SELECT amount FROM expense_approvals WHERE id = 1;
-- 0 + 6000 <= 10000
UPDATE expense_approvals SET approved = true WHERE id = 1;
COMMIT;
```

Session 2 (blocks on FOR UPDATE until Session 1 commits):
```sql
BEGIN;
SELECT total_budget FROM project_budgets
WHERE project_id = 1
FOR UPDATE;
-- waits here until Session 1 commits, then continues
SELECT COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0) AS approved_total
FROM expense_approvals
WHERE project_id = 1 AND approved = true;
-- now 6000 (sees Session 1's commit)
SELECT amount FROM expense_approvals WHERE id = 2;
-- 6000 + 5000 = 11000 > 10000 — refuse approval
ROLLBACK;
```

When SELECT FOR UPDATE works here vs a phantom INSERT case: the `project_budgets` row already exists, so FOR UPDATE locks it. Any concurrent approval that also locks the same row runs one at a time and re-checks the sum after the wait.

When SERIALIZABLE is preferable over SELECT FOR UPDATE: the lock target is unclear, you want the database to detect conflicts, or write contention is low enough that SSI abort/retry is acceptable. Under high contention on one project, FOR UPDATE gives more deterministic one-at-a-time throughput.

Comment: a table-level CHECK that approved totals stay under budget is not expressible as a simple row CHECK (it needs a multi-row aggregate). Trigger plus FOR UPDATE is a common production pattern; SERIALIZABLE is the cleaner conceptual fix for write skew.
